<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<lint>
    <!--
        Third-party bytecode, not this repository's source.

        `com.google.http-client:google-http-client:1.45.3` DOES reach the release
        runtime classpath (measured: `:app:dependencies --configuration
        releaseRuntimeClasspath` resolves it through
        `com.google.api-client:google-api-client-android:2.7.2`, which the Drive
        integration pulls in). So this is not dead test-only weight — it ships.

        The flagged code is `SslUtils$1` in that jar: an anonymous
        `javax.net.ssl.X509TrustManager` with empty `checkClientTrusted` and
        `checkServerTrusted` bodies, used by `SslUtils.trustAllSSLContext()`. It is a
        development helper baked into the artifact; we cannot fix it without patching
        or replacing the dependency, and replacing it is out of scope here.

        NEXO does not call `trustAllSSLContext()`. The app's TLS is configured through
        `network_security_config` plus the generated OkHttp client, neither of which
        uses this type. The finding is noise from a dependency's API surface, which is
        why it is suppressed here rather than accepted into the baseline.

        Scope: BOTH the path and the artifact must match, anchored to the Gradle
        USER HOME cache layout (`\.gradle/caches/modules-2/files-2.1/`) and to the
        exact artifact and version directory. No file under this repository can
        satisfy it: lint only scans our source roots and resolved dependency jars,
        and there is no `caches/modules-2` tree under the repo (the project-local
        `android/.gradle` holds build caches only). This cannot mask our own code.
    -->
    <issue id="TrustAllX509TrustManager">
        <ignore
            regexp="(?i).*[\\/]\.gradle[\\/]caches[\\/]modules-2[\\/]files-2\.1[\\/]com\.google\.http-client[\\/]google-http-client[\\/][^\\/]+[\\/][0-9a-f]+[\\/]google-http-client-[^\\/]+\.jar$" />
    </issue>
</lint>
